{"_type":"issue","id":"SlackBackup-2ut","title":"Tiered backup cadence: skip dormant/empty channels to cut nightly run time","description":"Nightly 'backup run' opens slackdump for all 389 tracked channels; each pays ~13-18s fixed overhead regardless of new data. ~38 min/run is spent re-wiping 0-message empties that download nothing (backup_logic.py:99-114). Introduce a MODIFIABLE cadence table mapping last-activity age -\u003e check interval (\u003c8wk nightly, 8-12wk every 2d, \u003e12wk \u0026 empties every 10d), keyed off catalog last_posted, with per-channel last_checked/last_action recorded in the catalog and deterministic staggering. Safe because max 10-day cadence sits far inside Slack's ~90-day retention. Drops dormant/empty per-night load ~249-\u003e~35 channels; empty-channel waste ~38min-\u003e~4min; active resumes unchanged.","design":"# Tiered backup cadence for dormant / empty channels\n\n## Problem\nThe nightly `backup run` opens slackdump for every tracked channel (389). Each\nchannel pays ~13–18s of fixed slackdump overhead (workspace select, session\nhandshake) regardless of whether there's anything to fetch. Measured on the\n2026-07-07 run: **~38 min** spent on 0-message channels alone, re-wiping and\nre-archiving empties that download nothing (`backup_logic.py:99-114`). Roughly\nhalf the run is spent confirming dormant/empty channels are still dormant/empty.\n\n## Real distribution (last message age, from archives, 2026-07-07)\n| Last activity | Channels |\n|---|---|\n| \u003c 4 wk | 175 |\n| 4–8 wk | 13 |\n| 8–12 wk | 23 |\n| 12–26 wk | 7 |\n| \u003e 26 wk | 0  (Slack ~90-day retention caps visible last-message age) |\n| empty / never posted | 219 |\n\nThe 249 dormant+empty channels are the target; the 188 active (\u003c8wk) stay nightly.\n\n## Design\n\n### Cadence table (MODIFIABLE — the whole point is this is easy to tune)\nA single ordered constant, first matching row wins, youngest→oldest. Empty /\nnever-posted channels have no last-activity and fall through to the oldest tier.\n\n```python\n# backup_logic.py (or a small config module)\n# (min_age_weeks, cadence_days) — first row whose min_age_weeks \u003c= channel age wins.\nBACKUP_CADENCE_TIERS = [\n    (0,  1),    # \u003c 8 wk  -\u003e every night\n    (8,  2),    # 8–12 wk -\u003e every other day\n    (12, 10),   # \u003e 12 wk -\u003e every 10 days\n]\n# Channels with no recorded last activity (empty / never posted) use the\n# LAST (oldest) tier's cadence.\n```\n\n### Dormancy signal\nKey off **last message timestamp**, NOT the archive file's mtime — mtime churns\nevery night (resume touches it, wipe rewrites it), so file-age would always read\nas \"fresh\". The catalog already persists `last_posted` per channel\n(`catalog_logic.update_last_posted`, written at `backup_logic.py:89/113/120`);\n`effective_recency` already reads it. Use that.\n\n### Master ledger = extend the existing catalog (not a new file)\nThe skip decision must be answerable WITHOUT opening the channel's sqlite,\notherwise skipping still costs a per-channel disk hit across all 389 archives.\nSo the catalog per-channel record must carry enough to decide cadence in memory:\n- `last_posted`   — already present; drives which tier the channel is in\n- `last_checked`  — NEW; drives whether the channel is due tonight\n- `last_action`   — NEW; `resume` | `archive` | `skip` (the \"what we found\" audit\n                    trail; optionally `last_duration_s`)\n\nOn a skip night the archive sqlite is never opened; the decision is a pure\ncatalog lookup. The sqlite is opened only when we actually back the channel up.\n\n### Staggering (no scheduler, no convergence night)\nDeterministic phase so a tier's members spread evenly across their interval:\n```\ndue tonight  iff  (hash(channel_id) mod cadence_days) == (epoch_day mod cadence_days)\n```\n`last_checked` is the belt-and-suspenders backstop: if the machine was off on a\nchannel's due night, the next run sees `last_checked` is older than the cadence\nand checks it anyway — so a missed night can never silently push a channel past\nSlack's retention window.\n\n### Retention safety (why this is provably safe)\nMax cadence is 10 days, far inside Slack's ~90-day free-tier retention. Even a\nyear-dormant channel that suddenly gets a burst of posts is re-checked within 10\ndays, while every one of those posts is still live on Slack's side. Backing off\ntherefore CANNOT lose messages; the only cost is detection latency (2–10 days),\nwhich is accepted for dormant channels.\n\n### Placement\nPure, unit-testable filter in `backup_logic.py`:\n`should_check_tonight(entry, catalog_record, today) -\u003e bool`\nconsulted by `backup_run` before the per-channel loop. `backup_run` records\n`last_checked`/`last_action` back into the catalog for every channel (checked or\nskipped). Matches the project's `*_logic.py` split; testable against a frozen date.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- A cadence table maps last-activity age → check interval and is a single\n  editable constant; changing it changes behavior with no other code edits.\n- Channels with recent activity (\u003c first threshold) are still checked nightly.\n- Dormant channels are still captured within their tier's cadence (verified with\n  a frozen `today` and synthetic `last_checked`/`last_posted`).\n- Empty / never-posted channels are assigned the oldest tier and skipped on\n  non-due nights (no slackdump invocation, no sqlite open on skip).\n- Checks are staggered so no single night processes an entire tier at once.\n- `last_checked` guarantees a channel skipped due to downtime is picked up on the\n  next run rather than drifting past its window.\n- Catalog records `last_checked` and `last_action` for every channel each run.\n- Measured effect: dormant/empty per-night load drops from ~249 channels to ~35;\n  the ~38 min empty-channel waste drops to roughly ~4 min. Active-channel work\n  (~188 nightly resumes) is unchanged.\n\n## Notes / risks\n- Corrupt/truncated sqlite reads as count 0 via the `sqlite3.Error -\u003e count = 0`\n  fallback (`backup_logic.py:94-97`) and would then be wiped, destroying a\n  damaged-but-partial archive. Out of scope here but worth a separate guard.\n- `-full` re-sync path (`backup_channel(full=True)`) is unaffected — always runs.\n","acceptance_criteria":"Cadence table is a single editable constant (age-\u003einterval). Active (\u003cfirst threshold) checked nightly. Dormant channels captured within their cadence (frozen-date test). Empty/never-posted -\u003e oldest tier, no slackdump/sqlite touch on skip nights. Checks staggered (no full-tier night). last_checked backstops downtime. Catalog records last_checked+last_action per channel per run. Measured: dormant/empty ~249-\u003e~35 per night, ~38min-\u003e~4min waste, active ~188 nightly resumes unchanged.","notes":"Implemented: BACKUP_CADENCE_TIERS constant + pure should_check_tonight(entry, record, today) filter in backup_logic.py; catalog_logic.record_check() stamps last_checked/last_action per channel per run. Wired into backup_logic.run() (new today param; -full bypasses filter). Staggering via stable sha1(channel_id) hash; last_checked downtime backstop. Tests: cadence tiers/boundaries, stagger coverage-within-cadence, backstop, run skip/record/full paths (tests/test_backup_logic.py), record_check (tests/test_catalog_logic.py). Steady-state sim on the issue's real distribution: dormant/empty ~34 checks/night (target ~35), 188 active stay nightly. Docs: DESIGN.md, OPERATIONS.md. 271 tests pass.","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"feature","assignee":"Stuart Donaldson","owner":"stuart.donaldson@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-07-07T20:16:50Z","created_by":"Stuart Donaldson","updated_at":"2026-07-07T20:43:33Z","started_at":"2026-07-07T20:30:05Z","closed_at":"2026-07-07T20:43:33Z","close_reason":"Closed","dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"SlackBackup-5df","title":"auth-refresh --keepalive: headless re-capture to keep slackdump in sync with Slack cookie rotation","description":"Slack rotates the session cookie forward (cookie attribute expiry is 2027 but slackdump sessions die in ~2-3 weeks); the browser follows the rotation, slackdump does not. Add a non-interactive --keepalive mode to refresh-auth.mjs: headless-launch the persistent profile, load app.slack.com to keep the session active / pick up rotation, re-capture the current shared cookie + tokens, and re-register all matched workspaces. Schedule it more often than Slack's inactivity window so slackdump never falls behind. Interactive 'npm run refresh' remains for initial login and hard re-auth.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA (frozen 2026-07-07):\nAC1: 'node refresh-auth.mjs --keepalive' runs fully non-interactively (no prompts), headless.\nAC2: it loads app.slack.com on the persistent profile to keep the session alive, then captures the current shared cookie + all team tokens once.\nAC3: it re-registers every tokens-file workspace whose team is present in the profile's localStorage (matchWorkspaceToken), using the single current cookie.\nAC4: it reports whether the cookie value rotated during the run (before vs after).\nAC5: EMPIRICAL: after a keepalive run, 'slackdump list channels -member-only -workspace \u003cws\u003e' validates for the re-registered workspaces (the whole point).\nAC6: reuses the existing pure logic (loadWorkspaces/parseXoxcTokens/pickSessionCookie/matchWorkspaceToken); no new untested branching logic of substance.","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"feature","owner":"stuart.donaldson@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-07-07T16:17:09Z","created_by":"Stuart Donaldson","updated_at":"2026-07-07T16:25:50Z","closed_at":"2026-07-07T16:25:50Z","close_reason":"Closed","comments":[{"id":"a75e04d8-71ab-5053-95a1-4697e767c91b","issue_id":"SlackBackup-5df","author":"Stuart Donaldson","text":"EMPIRICALLY VERIFIED. Hea