{"_type":"issue","id":"SlackBackup-d02","title":"fetch-files.sh: search-files harvesting across registered f3* workspaces","description":"Implements the file-harvesting half of docs/DESIGN-files.md (search-files\nside; pairs with build-file-index.sh for the indexing side).\n\nScope:\n- new scripts/fetch-files.sh \u003cout-archive-root\u003e [--terms-file \u003cpath\u003e]: loops\n  only currently-registered f3* workspaces (check against `slackdump\n  workspace list`); warns+skips (doesn't fail) any unregistered f3*\n  workspace, naming register-workspace.sh as the remedy. Per workspace, per\n  search term: `slackdump search files \u003cterm\u003e` into\n  \u003cout-archive-root\u003e/\u003cworkspace\u003e/\u003cterm\u003e/, then merge into one per-workspace\n  DB.\n- search terms externalized to scripts/config/file-search-terms.txt (one per\n  line, #-comments), default list: pax, convergence, help, ruck, ao, health.\n- new scripts/lib/fetch_files_helpers.sh: pure-logic pieces (term-file\n  parsing, registered-workspace filtering) split out for unit testing without\n  invoking slackdump.\n- new scripts/test_fetch_files.sh: live/manual, workspace-arg-gated (not\n  automated CI), plus unit tests of the helpers lib against fixtures.\n- Not wired into run-backups.sh — manual/occasional invocation only.\n\nBLOCKING PRECONDITION: empirically verify `slackdump tools merge` semantics\n(idempotent? additive? same-workspace-only?) before depending on it for the\nper-workspace merge step. Record findings as an addendum to\ndocs/references/slackdump-cli-notes.md's \"## tools merge\" section. Documented\nfallback if merge proves unsuitable: keep per-term result DBs separate and\nhave build-file-index.sh (SlackBackup-\u003cC-issue-id\u003e) read across all of them\ndirectly instead of one merged DB.\n\nAC:\n- Unregistered f3* workspaces skipped with a warning, not a failure.\n- Term list lives in editable config file, not hardcoded in the script.\n- tools merge semantics verified+documented before being relied on; fallback\n  documented if unsuitable.\n- Never invoked from run-backups.sh.\n\nDepends on: SlackBackup-6ql (workspace-iteration conventions land there\nfirst).\n\nSee docs/DESIGN-files.md and plan at\n/home/stuar/.claude/plans/explain-the-design-mcp-sleepy-moler.md","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"task","assignee":"Stuart Donaldson","owner":"stuart.donaldson@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-06-23T00:43:57Z","created_by":"Stuart Donaldson","updated_at":"2026-07-15T08:56:15Z","started_at":"2026-06-23T04:19:42Z","closed_at":"2026-06-23T04:24:37Z","close_reason":"Implemented: scripts/fetch-files.sh, scripts/lib/fetch_files_helpers.sh, scripts/config/file-search-terms.txt. Blocking precondition resolved empirically: slackdump tools merge cannot combine search-result DBs (empty CHANNEL table -\u003e 'getting channels: no data found'); documented in docs/references/slackdump-cli-notes.md. Adopted documented fallback: per-term result dirs kept separate, no merge step, build-file-index.sh (SlackBackup-t2b) reads across them directly. 6 passing unit tests (scripts/test_fetch_files.sh). Verified live against real f3* workspaces: registered workspaces harvested, unregistered ones skipped with correct register-workspace.sh remedy, idempotent re-run produces all skipped/no new API calls. Not wired into run-backups.sh.","dependencies":[{"issue_id":"SlackBackup-d02","depends_on_id":"SlackBackup-6ql","type":"blocks","created_at":"2026-06-22T17:44:16Z","created_by":"Stuart Donaldson","metadata":"{}"}],"dependency_count":1,"dependent_count":1,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"SlackBackup-6ql","title":"Channel catalog: generalize register-channel.sh into fast/full-tier catalog (single source of truth)","description":"Implements the channel-catalog half of docs/DESIGN-files.md.\n\nBinding design constraint: ONE catalog data file per workspace\n($CACHE_DIR/\u003cws\u003e.catalog.tsv, replacing the existing \u003cws\u003e.txt), columns id,\nname, member(yes/no), description. Fast tier (`list channels -member-only\n-format JSON`) upserts member=yes rows; full tier (`list channels -format\nJSON`, explicit/manual only) upserts remaining IDs as member=no, never\nclobbering an existing member=yes row. Both tiers parse JSON output (not the\ndefault Text table) so `topic`/`purpose` -\u003e `description` come from the same\nsingle API call, no extra query. No script outside the catalog-refresh\nfunctions may call `slackdump list channels` directly.\n\nScope:\n- scripts/register-channel.sh: rename cache file, add member+description\n  columns, add get_full_channel_list(), switch list parsing to JSON+jq, wire\n  full-tier fallback into existing name-lookup on a fast-tier miss.\n- new scripts/catalog-channels.sh \u003cworkspace\u003e [--full]: prints the catalog\n  dump (id, name, member, description, tracked, last_posted-for-tracked).\n- last-posted: local-only `SELECT MAX(ts) FROM MESSAGE` per tracked channel's\n  slackdump.sqlite, no API call.\n- run-backups.sh: one added call to warm the fast-tier cache per workspace\n  touched, fast tier only, never full.\n- delete stale channels-T*.txt (4 files in repo root) once verified replaced.\n- new scripts/test_catalog_channels.sh (fixture-driven, stubbed list\n  functions, asserts member-flag-never-downgraded + description parsing).\n\nAC:\n- Single catalog file per workspace; fast+full tiers both write into it.\n- Full tier never sets member=no over an existing member=yes row.\n- description populated from topic (fallback purpose) via -format JSON, same\n  call, no extra query.\n- No script outside catalog-refresh functions calls `slackdump list\n  channels` directly (grep -rn \"list channels\" scripts/ confirms this).\n- register-channel.sh name lookup falls back to full-tier refresh (not an\n  inline list channels call) on a fast-tier miss.\n- run-backups.sh warms only the fast tier, automatically, once per run.\n- Stale channels-T*.txt files removed.\n\nSee docs/DESIGN-files.md and plan at\n/home/stuar/.claude/plans/explain-the-design-mcp-sleepy-moler.md","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"task","assignee":"Stuart Donaldson","owner":"stuart.donaldson@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-06-23T00:43:43Z","created_by":"Stuart Donaldson","updated_at":"2026-07-15T08:56:15Z","started_at":"2026-06-23T00:44:34Z","closed_at":"2026-06-23T00:58:21Z","close_reason":"Implemented: scripts/lib/channel_catalog.sh (single catalog file/workspace, fast+full tiers, description via -format JSON same-call, no script outside this lib calls list channels directly), scripts/catalog-channels.sh, register-channel.sh refactored onto it, run-backups.sh fast-tier warm-up, stale channels-T*.txt removed, scripts/test_catalog_channels.sh (12 passing unit tests). Verified live: catalog refresh, name-based registration via fallback, tracked/last_posted cross-ref against real archives.","dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":2,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"SlackBackup-trb","title":"export_transform.sh: noise reduction + display_name resolution + late-reply-to-sealed-month fix","description":"## What changed\nCleaned up the monthly export schema for LLM-driven analysis of conversations/threads/\nlinks/references:\n- Reduced each message (parent and reply alike) to ts/user/display_name/text/files,\n  dropping Slack-API plumbing (blocks, client_msg_id, team, user_profile, metadata,\n  reply_count, reply_users, replace_original, etc.).\n- Resolved `user` (id, kept for cross-referencing) to a new `display_name` field using\n  the `users.json` that `slackdump convert -f export` writes alongside the day files\n  (profile.display_name -\u003e real_name -\u003e name -\u003e raw id fallback chain).\n- Handled bot/system messages that have no `user` field at all (use `bot_id`/`username`\n  instead) - this crashed jq with \"Cannot index object with null\" on a real archive\n  (f3cascades/otb-red-eye-reps) before the fix.\n- Fixed a related correctness gap surfaced while discussing the cross-month-thread\n  design with the user: sealing a month only means \"no new threads can start there\",\n  not \"no new replies can land there\" - Slack threads can get late replies long after\n  their parent's month was sealed and written. The skip-on-sealed logic now also\n  requires the freshly computed content to match what's on disk; a late reply now\n  reopens and rewrites the sealed month (logged as \"rewrote (late reply to sealed\n  month)\") instead of being silently dropped forever.","design":"See docs/DESIGN-export.md (Data Model + Crosscutting Concepts \u003e\u003e Idempotency sections, updated). Implementation in scripts/lib/export_transform.sh.","acceptance_criteria":"scripts/test_export_monthly.sh passes (13 cases incl. display_name resolution/fallback, bot-message handling, files reduction, noise stripping, and the late-reply-reopens-sealed-month scenario). Full re-export of all 15 tracked channels succeeds with no failures and no zero-byte files.","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"feature","owner":"stuart.donaldson@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-06-22T22:35:17Z","created_by":"Stuart Donaldson","updated_at":"2026-06-22T22:35:34Z","closed_at":"2026-06-22T22:35:34Z","close_reason":"Implemented in scripts/lib/export_transform.sh + scripts/test_export_monthly.sh + scripts/test_fixtures/export-archive/users.json, design notes in docs/DESIGN-export.md. Verified via test suite (13 passing cases) and a full re-export of all 15 real tracked channels.","dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"SlackBackup-ckz","title":"Implement scripts/export-monthly.sh monthly JSON exporter","description":"Implement the exporter designed in docs/DESIGN-export.md: read-only consumer of slackdump.sqlite that runs 'slackdump convert -f export', then transforms day-files into per-month \u003cws\u003e-\u003cslug\u003e-yyyy-mm.json with replies nested under their parent. Range bounds and month-bucketing key on the parent ts; threads stay whole in their parent's month. Idempotent: skip sealed months whose file exists, always rewrite the trailing (high-water) month. Seal predicate: .last_backup stamp if present else message high-water mark.","design":"Strategy B (read via documented export boundary). jq transform; GNU date for month math; sealed iff later-month data exists OR .last_backup\u003eend_of_month.","acceptance_criteria":"1) 1-root/2-reply thread -\u003e one top-level msg with replies[] in ts order, no reply at top level. 2) cross-month thread lands wholly in parent's month file. 3) --from/--to bounds threads by parent ts. 4) 3-month archive -\u003e 3 correctly-named files. 5) second run skips existing sealed months, bytes unchanged. 6) filename exactly \u003cws\u003e-\u003cslug\u003e-yyyy-mm.json. 7) exit 2 when archive missing.","notes":"Implemented: scripts/export-monthly.sh (entry point, I4 contract, exit 2 on missing archive), scripts/lib/export_transform.sh (range bound by parent ts, month bucketing, thread nesting, sealed-month idempotency with .last_backup stamp + high-water fallback). Fixtures in scripts/test_fixtures/export-archive/; tests in scripts/test_export_monthly.sh (test-plan items 1-6 + seal-stamp). Boundary (item 7) validated against real 'slackdump convert -f export' output: layout and verbatim field preservation confirmed. CONTEXT.md Core Capabilities + Non-Goals scope note updated. Seal-stamp write in backup.sh tracked by SlackBackup-026.","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"feature","assignee":"Stuart Donaldson","owner":"stuart.donaldson@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-06-22T19:55:10Z","created_by":"Stuart Donaldson","updated_at":"2026-06-22T20:03:26Z","started_at":"2026-06-22T19:55:14Z","closed_at":"2026-06-22T20:03:26Z","close_reason":"Exporter implemented and tested; all test-plan ACs pass, real-boundary round-trip validated.","dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"SlackBackup-026","title":"backup.sh: write durable .last_backup UTC stamp after successful archive/resume","description":"The monthly exporter (docs/DESIGN-export.md) seals a month as complete when last_backup \u003e end-of-month. backup.sh must write a git-durable \u003cchannel-dir\u003e/.last_backup (UTC, file content not mtime) after each successful archive/resume. Without it, the exporter falls back to the message high-water mark, which rewrites a quiet channel's last active month on every run.","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"task","owner":"stuart.donaldson@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-06-22T19:47:17Z","created_by":"Stuart Donaldson","updated_at":"2026-07-12T04:45:26Z","closed_at":"2026-07-12T04:45:26Z","close_reason":"Implemented: backup_logic._write_last_backup() writes a git-durable UTC \u003cchannel-dir\u003e/.last_backup stamp after each successful archive/resume path (full, first-archive, wipe+re-archive, resume). Exporter's seal predicate (DESIGN-export.md §Sealing signal) now gets the real stamp instead of the high-water-mark fallback. Tests added (test_backup_logic.py); full suite 276 passing. DESIGN-export.md updated to mark the stamp implemented.","dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
